ay":-0.1196,"pass_rate":0.5283,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5357,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1207},{"week":18,"opponent":"WAS","home":true,"pf":17.0,"pa":24.0,"off_snaps":62,"epa_per_play":-0.1029,"pass_rate":0.6935,"pass_rate_neutral":0.62,"def_epa_per_play":-0.02517}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2023,"team":"TB","distance":0.5533,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2025,"team":"IND","distance":0.6178,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Shane Steichen"},{"season":2023,"team":"NYG","distance":0.6368,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"PIT","distance":0.6524,"shared":21,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"JAX","distance":0.6658,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2023,"team":"MIA","distance":0.7075,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Vic Fangio"},{"season":2023,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.7448,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Jeff Ulbrich"},{"season":2024,"team":"ATL","distance":0.8026,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"LAC","distance":0.8936,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2020,"team":"TEN","distance":0.9396,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Eagles went 11-6 in 2025 and outscored opponents 379 to 325. The offense ranked 15th in EPA per play and the defense 8th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. What set them apart was the 2nd lowest screen rate and the highest Cover 1 rate in the league.","offense":"The Eagles offense was about average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 2nd lowest screen rate and the 2nd fewest backs in the backfield in the league. Jalen Hurts ran the passing game and leaned heavily on A.J. Brown, who drew 26% of the targets.","defense":"The Eagles had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 8th or better in three of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was EPA allowed per dropback, where it ranked 7th, and its weakest was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 18th. Nolan Smith was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Jalyx Hunt, who had 6.5 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. Schematically, they had the highest Cover 1 rate in the league.","special":"The Eagles were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 26% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 74% of field goals, which was one of the worst in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 19% of the money on the books. The offense also brought back more of its roster than most, with 88% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Jalen Hurts played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,224 yards. A.J. Brown was the clear top target with 26% of the looks and 1,003 receiving yards. Saquon Barkley handled most of the work in the backfield with 77% of the carries. On defense, Jalyx Hunt led the team with 6.5 sacks.","coaching":"Kevin Patullo called the offense and Vic Fangio called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Buccaneers under Dave Canales and the 2025 Colts under Shane Steichen. Their defense looked most like the 2023 Dolphins under Vic Fangio and the 2023 Jets under Jeff Ulbrich.","trends":"The offense went from 6th in EPA per play in 2024 to 15th in 2025, a big step back. The defense moved from 2nd in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 8th in 2025, a modest decline. The biggest change in approach was fourth down go rate, which went from 50% to 26%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}